# Week 4 Notes — Electronics and Amplifiers

## 1. Capacitors
A capacitor stores energy in an electric field.

- Charge: Q = CV
- Stored energy: E = 1/2 CV²
- Current-voltage relationship: I = C(dV/dt)
- RC time constant: τ = RC

During DC charging, current is initially highest and decreases as capacitor voltage approaches the supply. An ideal capacitor at steady-state DC behaves as an open circuit. Its voltage cannot change instantaneously.

| Time | Charging level | Discharging level |
|---|---:|---:|
| 1τ | 63.2% | 36.8% |
| 2τ | 86.5% | 13.5% |
| 3τ | 95.0% | 5.0% |
| 4τ | 98.2% | 1.8% |
| 5τ | 99.3% | 0.7% |

Practical capacitor checks: polarity, voltage rating, tolerance, leakage, ESR, ripple-current rating and temperature rating. Select voltage rating with margin above maximum operating voltage, including ripple and transients.

## 2. Diodes and LEDs
A diode conducts strongly when forward-biased and blocks current when reverse-biased, subject to leakage and reverse-breakdown limits. A silicon diode often has a forward drop around 0.6–0.7 V, depending on current and temperature.

An LED needs current limiting. A first-order resistor estimate is R = (Vs − Vf)/I. Use the LED datasheet or actual simulated operating point because the model's Vf may differ from an assumed value.

## 3. Bipolar Junction Transistor
For a BJT: IE = IC + IB. In forward-active operation, IC is approximately βIB. This relationship is not a universal current guarantee. In saturation, the external load and supply determine collector current.

- Cutoff: transistor off; collector current approximately zero.
- Forward-active: transistor can amplify.
- Saturation: transistor strongly on; collector-emitter voltage is low.

### NPN low-side switch
Connect the load between the positive supply and collector; connect emitter to circuit ground; drive the base through a resistor. A high control signal turns the transistor on, and a low signal turns it off. Use the circuit's 0 V reference as ground; do not confuse circuit ground with protective earth.

## 4. Common-emitter amplifier
A common-emitter stage uses the emitter as the common signal reference. A change in base-emitter drive changes collector current; the collector resistor converts that change into an output-voltage change.

VC = VCC − IC RC

As IC increases, the drop across RC increases and VC decreases. The output is inverted relative to the input (approximately 180° in the midband). The DC operating point (Q-point) should permit signal swing without early cutoff or saturation.

### Approximate divider bias
VB ≈ VCC × R2/(R1 + R2)
VE ≈ VB − VBE
IE ≈ VE/RE; IC ≈ IE
VC = VCC − IC RC
VCE = VC − VE

These are approximations; a more accurate design accounts for base current and divider loading.

## 5. Gain and frequency
Voltage-gain magnitude is |Av| = |Vout/Vin|, using the same amplitude convention for both signals. For an inverting common-emitter stage, signed gain is negative when phase inversion is confirmed.

Capacitive reactance is XC = 1/(2πfC). Increasing frequency decreases reactance for a fixed capacitor. Coupling and bypass capacitors affect low-frequency response. A few transient tests do not establish cutoff frequencies.

The -3 dB voltage-gain level is approximately 0.707 times midband gain.
